Groove 0.3 for RISC OS
============================

A music studio: drag loops into a song, play instruments
from the computer keyboard, draw beats and notes, record, and export a WAV.
Groove's own sounds are synthesised live: no samples to load.

Starting
  Double-click !Groove: it opens a new song (a drum track and a piano,
  ready to play) and the loops. Select on its icon bar icon opens the
  song window again; Adjust opens the loops. The icon bar menu has Info,
  Help, the User guide, the windows, New song and Quit.

User guide
  The full guide, with pictures, is !Groove.Guide.index/html: choose
  User guide from the icon bar menu to open it in your web browser.

The song window
  Toolbar   rewind, play/stop, record, cycle; bar.beat; tempo (Adjust =
            10 at a time); key; metronome; zoom; Loops, Edit, Keys;
            Vol, the master volume.
  Tracks    M mute, S solo, R record-arm; volume and pan sliders. Drag a
            track's name up or down to move it. Track > Rename in the
            menu names it. Tracks are coloured by kind of instrument.
            A song can have up to 32 tracks.
  Regions   drag to move, drag the right end to loop it longer; click to
            select (Delete removes it), double-click to edit. Double-click
            an empty track to make a new region. Ctrl-T splits the
            selected region at the playhead; Ctrl-C / Ctrl-V copy it and
            paste it at the playhead.
  Ruler     Select to move the playhead; Adjust-drag sets the cycle.
  The scroll bar along the bottom (or Shift + the scroll wheel) moves
  along the song; the track names stay put.

Keys (in any Groove window)
  Space     play / stop          R          record
  Return    back to the start    Left/Right a bar back / on
  A W S E D F T G Y H U J K O L P ;   play notes (A is C)
  Z / X     octave down / up     C / V      velocity down / up
  Ctrl-Z    undo                 Ctrl-Y     redo
  F7 loops  F8 editor  F9 keyboard  Ctrl-S save

Time signature
  Menu > Song > Time signature: 2/4 to 12/8, from the bar the playhead
  is in. The ruler shows where it changes; bars, the metronome and the
  count-in follow it.

MIDI keyboard
  A MIDI keyboard plays the selected track, like musical typing: with its
  velocity, recorded the same way, drum keys on drum tracks, and the
  sustain pedal. Two ways in:
  - MIDI Support (Pete Everett's !MidiSupp): Groove appears as a driver
    called "Groove". Connect your keyboard, interface or MIDIKeyboard to
    it in the MIDI Manager, or with *MIDIConnect <driver> Groove. Groove
    then listens whichever window has the input focus.
  - A MIDI module's ports (Rick Murray and Dave Hinton's USBMIDI, for USB
    MIDI keyboards and interfaces): Groove listens while one of its
    windows has the input focus.
  Menu > MIDI keyboard chooses MIDI Support, a port, or Off (MIDI Support
  to begin with, if it's loaded). The dot in the toolbar's display lights
  when notes come in.

Editor (double-click a region)
  Drums     a step grid. All keys shows all 128 MIDI drum keys with their
            General MIDI names; Kit rows the kit's eight, plus any other
            key the region uses.
  Notes     a piano roll of all 128 MIDI notes.
  Lane      the controller lane under the notes: its button chooses the
            controller (modulation, volume, pan, sustain, any CC, pitch
            bend, channel pressure, program change). Select puts an event
            (drag to draw), Adjust rubs out. Controllers play on MIDI out
            and SoundFont sounds, and go into MIDI files.

MIDI out
  Track > Play on MIDI out (F11): the track is played by a MIDI
  instrument (a keyboard or module on USB or a MIDI cable, or a software
  synth like MidiSynth) instead of by Groove: its notes, sound, volume
  and pan.
  Song > Play on MIDI out (Shift-F11) does every drum and instrument
  track at once.
  Menu > MIDI out chooses where: MIDI Support (connect "Groove" to the
  instrument in the MIDI Manager, or *MIDIConnect Groove <name>), a MIDI
  module's port, or Off (Groove plays those tracks itself). Song notes go
  out in time with Groove's own sound; stopping sends All Notes Off.
  F10 steps through the MIDI out choices (Off, MIDI Support, Port 1-4);
  the song window's title shows where MIDI out goes ("MIDI out: Support"),
  and is just the song's name when it's Off.
  Track > MIDI channel (Auto, 1-16) and Track > Bank & program (Bank MSB,
  Bank LSB, Program; empty for Groove's choice) set what's sent.

Recording
  R counts in a bar first (Count-in in the main menu turns this off).
  With Merge recordings ticked, what you record over a region goes into
  it, instead of into a new region.

The editor
  Drums: Select turns a step on or off (drag along a row to paint);
  Adjust accents it. Piano roll: Select draws a note (drag to lengthen) or
  moves one; Adjust deletes. Shift-click adds a note to the selection, and
  Shift-drag on empty space selects with a box; drag a selected note to
  move them all. With notes selected: Up/Down transpose (with Shift, an
  octave), Ctrl-Up/Down louder/softer, Delete, Ctrl-C / Ctrl-V copy and
  paste (at the playhead), Ctrl-A selects all, Escape none. Brighter
  notes are louder. The coloured button changes the instrument or kit.

SoundFont sounds (with !SoftMIDI)
  If !SoftMIDI is installed (from PackMan), Track > Sound (or the
  editor's sound button) also offers SoundFont > family > instrument, and
  SoundFont kits for drum tracks: the 128 General MIDI sounds of
  !SoftMIDI's SoundFont. It loads the first time you choose one (a few
  seconds). Without !SoftMIDI those tracks play Groove's nearest sound.

Files
  Save (Ctrl-S or Menu > Song > Save), Export as WAV and Export as MIDI
  use a normal save box: drag the icon to a directory. Drop a song, a WAV,
  a MIDI file or Maestro music on Groove's icon or song window to load or
  import it (MIDI and Maestro files become a track per part or channel,
  from the bar you drop them on).

Maestro music
  Groove plays Maestro files as Maestro does: clefs, key signatures,
  accidentals to the end of the bar, ties, dots, each channel's volume and
  stereo position, and (into an empty song) the tempo, key and time
  signatures. Each channel's voice gets the nearest SoundFont sound.

Requirements: RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with VFP (a Raspberry Pi 2 or
later); SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport from PackMan.
Recommended: SoftMIDI from PackMan, for SoundFont sounds.

